                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Tedious and boring solitaire, with 400 levels, most of which are not balanced to be played. \n\nMeaning, you get a set of 10 levels (a chapter), and you just skip through them, clicking the deck from the first card to the last, but keeping in mind 'the goals', like, achieving perfects, getting stars and gold, and so on. \n\nMeaning, you either try to beat every level on the first try, and fail miserably half the time... Or you skip from level x-1 to x-9 (not touching x-10, though), then walk back and pick the easiest levels to hit the goals.\n\nThat alone is a game development choice I’d label as bad and shameful. Forced backtracking in a solitaire, what's worse? Well, there is also a UI that is quite bad in many aspects. In the main menu, the \"Continue\" button is so thin and close to \"New Game\" that every time you want to play, you risk starting this marathon of boredom all over again. There are also issues with the in-game store, where you have to buy fire-extinguishing potions between levels, and companions that don't scale the way anyone would want (the dervish is way more useful than the dwarf).\n\n All in all, it's probably not the worst solitaire game on the planet, but it's well below the level of decency. Like, 5/10 mark.",
